$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">In the figure above, <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_001_74a28996.png" alt="line segment M Q "></span></span> and <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_002_6aed6348.png" alt="line segment N R "></span></span> intersect at point <span class="italic">P</span>, <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_003_56691505.png" alt="N P equals Q P"></span></span>, and <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_004_fbcf9070.png" alt="M P equals P R"></span></span>. What is the measure, in degrees, of <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_005_d7b02910.png" alt="angle Q M R"></span></span> ? (Disregard the degree symbol when gridding your answer.)</p>
$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["30"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is 30. It is given that the measure of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_006_1c87ca04.png" alt="angle Q P R"></span> is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_007_1725b68a.png" alt="60 degrees"></span>. Angle <span class="italic">MPR</span> and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_008_1c87ca04.png" alt="angle Q P R"></span> are collinear and therefore are supplementary angles. This means that the sum of the two angle measures is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_009_429202ed.png" alt="180 degrees"></span>, and so the measure of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_010_b459413d.png" alt="angle M P R"></span> is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_011_8c0f207c.png" alt="120 degrees"></span>. The sum of the angles in a triangle is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_012_429202ed.png" alt="180 degrees"></span>. Subtracting the measure of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_013_b459413d.png" alt="angle M P R"></span> from <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_014_429202ed.png" alt="180 degrees"></span> yields the sum of the other angles in the triangle MPR. Since <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_015_4f8903a2.png" alt="180 minus 120, equals 60"></span>, the sum of the measures of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_016_d7b02910.png" alt="angle Q M R"></span> and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_017_3b49ae8f.png" alt="angle N R M"></span> is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_018_1725b68a.png" alt="60 degrees"></span>. It is given that <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_019_fbcf9070.png" alt="the length of side M P equals the length of side P R"></span>, so it follows that triangle <span class="italic">MPR</span> is isosceles. Therefore<span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_020_d7b02910.png" alt="angle Q M R"></span> and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_021_3b49ae8f.png" alt="angle N R M"></span> must be congruent. Since the sum of the measure of these two angles is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_022_1725b68a.png" alt="60 degrees"></span>, it follows that the measure of each angle is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_023_44dd7f9a.png" alt="30 degrees"></span>.<p>An alternate approach would be to use the exterior angle theorem, noting that the measure of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_024_1c87ca04.png" alt="angle Q P R"></span> is equal to the sum of the measures of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_025_d7b02910.png" alt="angle Q M R"></span> and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_026_3b49ae8f.png" alt="angle N R M"></span>. Since both angles are equal, each of them has a measure of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_027_44dd7f9a.png" alt="30 degrees"></span>.</p></p>
$SATQ$, true, 28, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$SPR$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"947a3cde","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"05757-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.B.","skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"spr","stimulus":"<div xmlns=\"http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1\" class=\"stimulus_reference \">\n        <div class=\"standalone_image \">\n          <img src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_000_bd087203.png\" alt=\"The figure presents line segments MQ and NR that intersect at point P, where point N is above and slightly to the right of point M and point Q is above and slightly to the left of point R. Line segments MN, QR, and horizontal line segment M, R are drawn forming triangles MNR and QMR. The measure of angle QPR is labeled 60 degrees and the angle measure of MQR is labeled 70 degrees.\" width=\"143\" height=\"81\">\n        </div>\n      </div>\n","stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">In the figure above, <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_001_74a28996.png\" alt=\"line segment M Q \"></span></span> and <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_002_6aed6348.png\" alt=\"line segment N R \"></span></span> intersect at point <span class=\"italic\">P</span>, <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_003_56691505.png\" alt=\"N P equals Q P\"></span></span>, and <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_004_fbcf9070.png\" alt=\"M P equals P R\"></span></span>. What is the measure, in degrees, of <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_005_d7b02910.png\" alt=\"angle Q M R\"></span></span> ? (Disregard the degree symbol when gridding your answer.)</p>\n","answerOptions":null,"keys":null,"correct_answer":["30"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is 30. It is given that the measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_006_1c87ca04.png\" alt=\"angle Q P R\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_007_1725b68a.png\" alt=\"60 degrees\"></span>. Angle <span class=\"italic\">MPR</span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_008_1c87ca04.png\" alt=\"angle Q P R\"></span> are collinear and therefore are supplementary angles. This means that the sum of the two angle measures is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_009_429202ed.png\" alt=\"180 degrees\"></span>, and so the measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_010_b459413d.png\" alt=\"angle M P R\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_011_8c0f207c.png\" alt=\"120 degrees\"></span>. The sum of the angles in a triangle is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_012_429202ed.png\" alt=\"180 degrees\"></span>. Subtracting the measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_013_b459413d.png\" alt=\"angle M P R\"></span> from <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_014_429202ed.png\" alt=\"180 degrees\"></span> yields the sum of the other angles in the triangle MPR. Since <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_015_4f8903a2.png\" alt=\"180 minus 120, equals 60\"></span>, the sum of the measures of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_016_d7b02910.png\" alt=\"angle Q M R\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_017_3b49ae8f.png\" alt=\"angle N R M\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_018_1725b68a.png\" alt=\"60 degrees\"></span>. It is given that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_019_fbcf9070.png\" alt=\"the length of side M P equals the length of side P R\"></span>, so it follows that triangle <span class=\"italic\">MPR</span> is isosceles. Therefore<span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_020_d7b02910.png\" alt=\"angle Q M R\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_021_3b49ae8f.png\" alt=\"angle N R M\"></span> must be congruent. Since the sum of the measure of these two angles is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_022_1725b68a.png\" alt=\"60 degrees\"></span>, it follows that the measure of each angle is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_023_44dd7f9a.png\" alt=\"30 degrees\"></span>.<p>An alternate approach would be to use the exterior angle theorem, noting that the measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_024_1c87ca04.png\" alt=\"angle Q P R\"></span> is equal to the sum of the measures of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_025_d7b02910.png\" alt=\"angle Q M R\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_026_3b49ae8f.png\" alt=\"angle N R M\"></span>. Since both angles are equal, each of them has a measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/947a3cde/img_027_44dd7f9a.png\" alt=\"30 degrees\"></span>.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"SPR","section":"Math","image_count":28,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959630,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"947a3cde","skill_cd":"S.B.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","createDate":1691007959630,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"05757-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"12afd426-90e4-4d60-abc3-1d75c8f272e3","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"05757-DC","section":"Math","body":"<div xmlns=\"http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1\" class=\"stimulus_reference \">\n        <div class=\"standalone_image \">\n          <img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"The figure presents line segments MQ and NR that intersect at point P, where point N is above and slightly to the right of point M and point Q is above and slightly to the left of point R. Line segments MN, QR, and horizontal line segment M, R are drawn forming triangles MNR and QMR. The measure of angle QPR is labeled 60 degrees and the angle measure of MQR is labeled 70 degrees.\" width=\"143\" height=\"81\">\n        </div>\n      </div>\n","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">In the figure above, <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"line segment M Q \"></span></span> and <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"line segment N R \"></span></span> intersect at point <span class=\"italic\">P</span>, <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"N P equals Q P\"></span></span>, and <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"M P equals P R\"></span></span>. What is the measure, in degrees, of <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle Q M R\"></span></span> ? (Disregard the degree symbol when gridding your answer.)</p>\n","answer":{"style":"SPR","rationale":"<p>The correct answer is 30. It is given that the measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle Q P R\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"60 degrees\"></span>. Angle <span class=\"italic\">MPR</span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle Q P R\"></span> are collinear and therefore are supplementary angles. This means that the sum of the two angle measures is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"180 degrees\"></span>, and so the measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle M P R\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"120 degrees\"></span>. The sum of the angles in a triangle is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"180 degrees\"></span>. Subtracting the measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle M P R\"></span> from <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"180 degrees\"></span> yields the sum of the other angles in the triangle MPR. Since <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"180 minus 120, equals 60\"></span>, the sum of the measures of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle Q M R\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle N R M\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"60 degrees\"></span>. It is given that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the length of side M P equals the length of side P R\"></span>, so it follows that triangle <span class=\"italic\">MPR</span> is isosceles. Therefore<span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle Q M R\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle N R M\"></span> must be congruent. Since the sum of the measure of these two angles is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"60 degrees\"></span>, it follows that the measure of each angle is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"30 degrees\"></span>.<p>An alternate approach would be to use the exterior angle theorem, noting that the measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle Q P R\"></span> is equal to the sum of the measures of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle Q M R\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle N R M\"></span>. Since both angles are equal, each of them has a measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"30 degrees\"></span>.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959630,"updateDate":1691007959630,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959630, 1691007959630),
