t-Regular-97\"></use>\n     <use x=\"799.414062\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-119\"></use>\n     <use x=\"871.09375\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-110\"></use>\n     <use x=\"924.121094\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-32\"></use>\n     <use x=\"946.484375\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-116\"></use>\n     <use x=\"977.148438\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-111\"></use>\n     <use x=\"1026.953125\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-32\"></use>\n     <use x=\"1049.316406\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-115\"></use>\n     <use x=\"1084.375\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-99\"></use>\n     <use x=\"1123.925781\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-97\"></use>\n     <use x=\"1165.234375\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-108\"></use>\n     <use x=\"1189.746094\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-101\"></use>\n     <use x=\"1231.738281\" xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-46\"></use>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n  </g>\n </g>\n</g>\n    <g transform=\"translate(23, 18) scale(1 1) \"><polygon points=\"192.491 0.945 192.491 146.149 0.945 146.149 192.491 0.945\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"1.89\"></polygon>\n    <rect x=\"181.151\" y=\"134.809\" width=\"11.34\" height=\"11.34\" stroke-width=\"0.945\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" fill=\"none\"></rect>\n    <style>\n        .small {\n            font: italic 13px Crimson;\n        }\n\n        .heavy {\n            font: bold 30px sans-serif;\n        }\n\n        /* Note that the color of the text is set with the    *\n            * fill property, the color property is for HTML only */\n        .Rrrrr {\n            font: italic 40px serif;\n            fill: red;\n        }\n    </style>\n</g>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n<div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for right triangle upper Q upper R upper S\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul><li>Angle upper R is a right angle.</li>\n<li>The length of side upper R upper S is 18.</li>\n<li>A note indicates the figure is not drawn to scale.</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R upper S\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> shown, <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R less than upper R upper S\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math>. Which expression represents the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper Q upper S\"><mover><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>?</p>","keys":["d2105737-7034-498a-9663-2fce311dc9a6"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"bb5cb995-3620-4a77-9bc2-f243d28713ed","content":"<p><math alttext=\"18 cosine upper Q\"><mn>18</mn><mi>cos</mi><mi>Q</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"140c2c48-2992-42d2-9a1a-29643dc73eb2","content":"<p><math alttext=\"18 sine upper Q\"><mn>18</mn><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"62596835-e9ab-40fd-bab9-9e9eeb3d2258","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 18 Over cosine upper Q EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>18</mn><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"d2105737-7034-498a-9663-2fce311dc9a6","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 18 Over sine upper Q EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>18</mn><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. The figure shows that triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q upper R upper S\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> is a right triangle. Each of the given choices is an expression containing&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"sine upper Q\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"cosine upper Q\"><mi>cos</mi><mi>Q</mi></math>. For an acute angle in a right triangle, the sine of the angle is the length of the opposite leg divided by the length of the hypotenuse, and the cosine of the angle is the length of the adjacent leg divided by the length of the hypotenuse. It follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"sine upper Q equals StartFraction upper R upper S Over upper Q upper S EndFraction\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"cosine upper Q equals StartFraction upper Q upper R Over upper Q upper S EndFraction\"><mi>cos</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q upper R less than upper R upper S\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math>. It's given in the figure that the length of side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R upper S\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn></math>. Since only the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"sine upper Q equals StartFraction upper R upper S Over upper Q upper S EndFraction\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R upper S\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math>, an expression representing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q upper S\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></math> can be found by substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R upper S\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> in this equation, which yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"sine upper Q equals StartFraction 18 Over upper Q upper S EndFraction\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>18</mn><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q upper S\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q upper S dot sine upper Q equals 18\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>&#183;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"sine upper Q\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q upper S equals StartFraction 18 Over sine upper Q EndFraction\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>18</mn><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. Therefore, the expression <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 18 Over sine upper Q EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>18</mn><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> represents the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper Q upper S\"><menclose notation=\"top\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></menclose></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","externalid":"60ba6bab-7d16-46f1-9882-e80e2dd3577c","correct_answer":["D"]},"createDate":1776362727992,"updateDate":1776362727992}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362727992, 1776362727992),
    ($SATQ$95ba2d09$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$025648-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Circles$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<div class="stimulus_reference "><div class="standalone_image "><img src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_004_36673712.png" alt="The figure presents a circle in the x y plane, with the origin labeled O. The center of the circle lies at the origin. There are 4 points, T, R, Q, and P, labeled clockwise on the circle. Point T lies on the x axis, directly to the left of the origin, and has coordinates negative 1 comma 0. Point R lies above the x axis, and to the left of the y axis. Point Q lies above the x axis and to the right of the y axis. Point P lies on the x axis, directly to the right of the origin, and has coordinates 1 comma 0. Points R and Q appear to be symmetric with respect to the y axis. Four line segments are drawn from the origin to each of the four points on the circle." width="151" height="114"></div></div>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">In the <span class="italic ">xy</span>-plane above, points <span class="italic">P</span>, <span class="italic">Q</span>, <span class="italic">R</span>, and <span class="italic">T</span> lie on the circle with center <span class="italic">O</span>. The degree measures of angles <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_005_e52edd05.png" alt="P O Q "></span></span> and <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_006_d42fc126.png" alt="R O T "></span></span> are each 30&deg;. What is the <u><span class="underline ">radian</span></u> measure of angle <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_007_a0fd7207.png" alt="Q O R "></span></span> ?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_000_070f3ddd.png\" alt=\"five sixths, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_001_8fa38561.png\" alt=\"three fourths, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_002_6e93ab86.png\" alt=\"two thirds, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_003_9788e124.png\" alt=\"one third, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is correct. Because points <span class="italic">T</span>, <span class="italic">O</span>, and <span class="italic">P</span> all lie on the <span class="italic">x</span>-axis, they form a line. Since the angles on a line add up to <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_008_429202ed.png" alt="180 degrees"></span>, and i<span class="tcp-fdcb240c-dfdc-45de-b3ba-7a1fc77c55b6">t&rsquo;s given that angles <span class="italic">POQ</span> and <span class="italic">ROT</span> each measure </span><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_009_44dd7f9a.png" alt="30 degrees "></span><span class="tcp-f8277a6b-e06a-4650-ac4f-4fdd9dab4c6d">, it follows that the measure of angle <i>QOR </i></span>is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_010_15a4b67a.png" alt="180 degrees minus 30 degrees, minus 30 degrees, equals 120 degrees"></span>. Since the arc of a complete circle is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_011_a8b0a5d1.png" alt="360 degrees"></span> or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_012_af1d8b8a.png" alt="2 pi"></span> radians, a proportion can be set up to convert the measure of angle <span class="italic">QOR </span>from <span class="tcp-f9772afb-4b75-4f3e-a8bd-df7343bc360b">degrees to radians: <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_013_f99a7847.png" alt="the fraction 360 degrees over 2 pi radians, equals, the fraction 120 degrees over x radians"></span>, where <span class="italic">x</span> is the radian measure of angle <span class="italic">QOR</span>. Multiplying each side of the proportion by </span><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_014_b720d53b.png" alt="2 pi x "></span><span class="tcp-0cf4ffe6-1795-44f7-bb24-c305869b5c4e"> gives </span><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_015_41b91f0e.png" alt="360 x equals 240 pi "></span><span class="tcp-14300223-9ad6-44a1-aeb4-8fa749b6c278">. Solving for </span><span class="italic tcp-b1a95783-9bdf-4e36-9679-128e646e2f7c">x</span><span class="tcp-6553f588-aed9-4193-b1de-cad894d30bdc"> gives </span><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_016_dfa28076.png" alt="the fraction 240 over 360 times pi"></span><span class="tcp-50a9d5d5-a676-4c6e-8d47-73037b6ef81f">, or </span><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_017_6e93ab86.png" alt="two thirds pi"></span><span class="tcp-f40007e3-a816-4c96-8d96-b176771f13ca">.</span><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from subtracting only angle <span class="italic">POQ</span> from <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_018_429202ed.png" alt="180 degrees "></span>to get a value of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_019_a24982ba.png" alt="150 degrees "></span>and then finding the radian measure equivalent to that value. Choice B is incorrect and may result from a calculation error. Choice D is incorrect and may result from calculating the sum of the angle measures, in radians, of angles <span class="italic">POQ </span>and <span class="italic">ROT</span>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 20, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"95ba2d09","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"025648-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.D.","skill_desc":"Circles","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference \"><div class=\"standalone_image \"><img src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_004_36673712.png\" alt=\"The figure presents a circle in the x y plane, with the origin labeled O. The center of the circle lies at the origin. There are 4 points, T, R, Q, and P, labeled clockwise on the circle. Point T lies on the x axis, directly to the left of the origin, and has coordinates negative 1 comma 0. Point R lies above the x axis, and to the left of the y axis. Point Q lies above the x axis and to the right of the y axis. Point P lies on the x axis, directly to the right of the origin, and has coordinates 1 comma 0. Points R and Q appear to be symmetric with respect to the y axis. Four line segments are drawn from the origin to each of the four points on the circle.\" width=\"151\" height=\"114\"></div></div>\n","stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">In the <span class=\"italic \">xy</span>-plane above, points <span class=\"italic\">P</span>, <span class=\"italic\">Q</span>, <span class=\"italic\">R</span>, and <span class=\"italic\">T</span> lie on the circle with center <span class=\"italic\">O</span>. The degree measures of angles <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_005_e52edd05.png\" alt=\"P O Q \"></span></span> and <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_006_d42fc126.png\" alt=\"R O T \"></span></span> are each 30&deg;. What is the <u><span class=\"underline \">radian</span></u> measure of angle <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_007_a0fd7207.png\" alt=\"Q O R \"></span></span> ?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_000_070f3ddd.png\" alt=\"five sixths, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_001_8fa38561.png\" alt=\"three fourths, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_002_6e93ab86.png\" alt=\"two thirds, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_003_9788e124.png\" alt=\"one third, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. Because points <span class=\"italic\">T</span>, <span class=\"italic\">O</span>, and <span class=\"italic\">P</span> all lie on the <span class=\"italic\">x</span>-axis, they form a line. Since the angles on a line add up to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_008_429202ed.png\" alt=\"180 degrees\"></span>, and i<span class=\"tcp-fdcb240c-dfdc-45de-b3ba-7a1fc77c55b6\">t&rsquo;s given that angles <span class=\"italic\">POQ</span> and <span class=\"italic\">ROT</span> each measure </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_009_44dd7f9a.png\" alt=\"30 degrees \"></span><span class=\"tcp-f8277a6b-e06a-4650-ac4f-4fdd9dab4c6d\">, it follows that the measure of angle <i>QOR </i></span>is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_010_15a4b67a.png\" alt=\"180 degrees minus 30 degrees, minus 30 degrees, equals 120 degrees\"></span>. Since the arc of a complete circle is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_011_a8b0a5d1.png\" alt=\"360 degrees\"></span> or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_012_af1d8b8a.png\" alt=\"2 pi\"></span> radians, a proportion can be set up to convert the measure of angle <span class=\"italic\">QOR </span>from <span class=\"tcp-f9772afb-4b75-4f3e-a8bd-df7343bc360b\">degrees to radians: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_013_f99a7847.png\" alt=\"the fraction 360 degrees over 2 pi radians, equals, the fraction 120 degrees over x radians\"></span>, where <span class=\"italic\">x</span> is the radian measure of angle <span class=\"italic\">QOR</span>. Multiplying each side of the proportion by </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_014_b720d53b.png\" alt=\"2 pi x \"></span><span class=\"tcp-0cf4ffe6-1795-44f7-bb24-c305869b5c4e\"> gives </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_015_41b91f0e.png\" alt=\"360 x equals 240 pi \"></span><span class=\"tcp-14300223-9ad6-44a1-aeb4-8fa749b6c278\">. Solving for </span><span class=\"italic tcp-b1a95783-9bdf-4e36-9679-128e646e2f7c\">x</span><span class=\"tcp-6553f588-aed9-4193-b1de-cad894d30bdc\"> gives </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_016_dfa28076.png\" alt=\"the fraction 240 over 360 times pi\"></span><span class=\"tcp-50a9d5d5-a676-4c6e-8d47-73037b6ef81f\">, or </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_017_6e93ab86.png\" alt=\"two thirds pi\"></span><span class=\"tcp-f40007e3-a816-4c96-8d96-b176771f13ca\">.</span><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from subtracting only angle <span class=\"italic\">POQ</span> from <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_018_429202ed.png\" alt=\"180 degrees \"></span>to get a value of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/95ba2d09/img_019_a24982ba.png\" alt=\"150 degrees \"></span>and then finding the radian measure equivalent to that value. Choice B is incorrect and may result from a calculation error. Choice D is incorrect and may result from calculating the sum of the angle measures, in radians, of angles <span class=\"italic\">POQ </span>and <span class=\"italic\">ROT</span>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":20,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959619,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"95ba2d09","skill_cd":"S.D.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Circles","createDate":1691007959619,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"025648-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"b8e65f4f-03bc-4883-8a68-666a3f65d9cd","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"025648-DC","section":"Math","body":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference \"><div class=\"standalone_image \"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"The figure presents a circle in the x y plane, with the origin labeled O. The center of the circle lies at the origin. There are 4 points, T, R, Q, and P, labeled clockwise on the circle. Point T lies on the x axis, directly to the left of the origin, and has coordinates negative 1 comma 0. Point R lies above the x axis, and to the left of the y axis. Point Q lies above the x axis and to the right of the y axis. Point P lies on the x axis, directly to the right of the origin, and has coordinates 1 comma 0. Points R and Q appear to be symmetric with respect to the y axis. Four line segments are drawn from the origin to each of the four points on the circle.\" width=\"151\" height=\"114\"></div></div>\n","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">In the <span class=\"italic \">xy</span>-plane above, points <span class=\"italic\">P</span>, <span class=\"italic\">Q</span>, <span class=\"italic\">R</span>, and <span class=\"italic\">T</span> lie on the circle with center <span class=\"italic\">O</span>. The degree measures of angles <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"P O Q \"></span></span> and <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"R O T \"></span></span> are each 30&deg;. What is the <u><span class=\"underline \">radian</span></u> measure of angle <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Q O R \"></span></span> ?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"five sixths, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"three fourths, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"two thirds, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one third, pi\"></span></span></p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"c","rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. Because points <span class=\"italic\">T</span>, <span class=\"italic\">O</span>, and <span class=\"italic\">P</span> all lie on the <span class=\"italic\">x</span>-axis, they form a line. Since the angles on a line add up to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"180 degrees\"></span>, and i<span class=\"tcp-fdcb240c-dfdc-45de-b3ba-7a1fc77c55b6\">t&rsquo;s given that angles <span class=\"italic\">POQ</span> and <span class=\"italic\">ROT</span> each measure </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"30 degrees \"></span><span class=\"tcp-f8277a6b-e06a-4650-ac4f-4fdd9dab4c6d\">, it follows that the measure of angle <i>QOR </i></span>is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"180 degrees minus 30 degrees, minus 30 degrees, equals 120 degrees\"></span>. Since the arc of a complete circle is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"360 degrees\"></span> or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 pi\"></span> radians, a proportion can be set up to convert the measure of angle <span class=\"italic\">QOR </span>from <span class=\"tcp-f9772afb-4b75-4f3e-a8bd-df7343bc360b\">degrees to radians: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 360 degrees over 2 pi radians, equals, the fraction 120 degrees over x radians\"></span>, where <span class=\"italic\">x</span> is the radian measure of angle <span class=\"italic\">QOR</span>. Multiplying each side of the proportion by </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 pi x \"></span><span class=\"tcp-0cf4ffe6-1795-44f7-bb24-c305869b5c4e\"> gives </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"360 x equals 240 pi \"></span><span class=\"tcp-14300223-9ad6-44a1-aeb4-8fa749b6c278\">. Solving for </span><span class=\"italic tcp-b1a95783-9bdf-4e36-9679-128e646e2f7c\">x</span><span class=\"tcp-6553f588-aed9-4193-b1de-cad894d30bdc\"> gives </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 240 over 360 times pi\"></span><span class=\"tcp-50a9d5d5-a676-4c6e-8d47-73037b6ef81f\">, or </span><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"two thirds pi\"></span><span class=\"tcp-f40007e3-a816-4c96-8d96-b176771f13ca\">.</span><p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from subtracting only angle <span class=\"italic\">POQ</span> from <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"180 degrees \"></span>to get a value of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"150 degrees \"></span>and then finding the radian measure equivalent to that value. Choice B is incorrect and may result from a calculation error. Choice D is incorrect and may result from calculating the sum of the angle measures, in radians, of angles <span class=\"italic\">POQ </span>and <span class=\"italic\">ROT</span>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959619,"updateDate":1691007959619}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959619, 1691007959619),
