</math> and <math alttext="upper L"><mi>L</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0d7e7549-c57b-41a3-a116-f4927b8b1106","content":"<p><math alttext=\"14 pi\"><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"d8283e43-3819-4c46-88c1-b80cb5e74b1e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"28 pi\"><mrow><mn>28</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"407365b3-0320-4dcc-bf16-f563229514d2","content":"<p><math alttext=\"56 pi\"><mrow><mn>56</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"a983ec24-94db-48a5-babe-ffc256daa98a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"116 pi\"><mrow><mn>116</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a983ec24-94db-48a5-babe-ffc256daa98a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is correct. The area, <math alttext="upper A"><mi>A</mi>
</math>, of a circle is given by the formula <math alttext="upper A equals pi r squared"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&pi;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, where <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math> represents the radius of the circle. It&rsquo;s given that circle <math alttext="upper K"><mi>K</mi>
</math> has a radius of <math alttext="4 millimeters left parenthesis mm right parenthesis"><mn>4</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>millimeters</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mfenced><mtext>mm</mtext></mfenced></math>. Substituting <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math> in the formula <math alttext="upper A equals pi r squared"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&pi;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> yields <math alttext="upper A equals pi left parenthesis 4 right parenthesis squared"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&pi;</mi><msup><mfenced><mn>4</mn></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext="upper A equals 16 pi"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>&pi;</mi></math>. Therefore, the area of circle <math alttext="upper K"><mi>K</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="16 pi mm Superscript 2"><mn>16</mn><mi>&pi;</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mtext>mm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. It&rsquo;s given that circle <math alttext="upper L"><mi>L</mi>
</math> has an area of <math alttext="100 pi mm Superscript 2"><mn>100</mn><mi>&pi;</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mtext>mm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Therefore, the total area,&nbsp;<math alttext="in mm Superscript 2"><msup><mtext>in&nbsp;mm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, of circles <math alttext="upper K"><mi>K</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="upper L"><mi>L</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="16 pi plus 100 pi"><mn>16</mn><mi>&pi;</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>&pi;</mi></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="116 pi"><mn>116</mn><mi>&pi;</mi></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. This is the sum of the radii,&nbsp;<math alttext="in mm"><mtext>in&nbsp;mm</mtext></math>, of circles <math alttext="upper K"><mi>K</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="upper L"><mi>L</mi>
</math> multiplied by <math alttext="pi"><mi>&pi;</mi></math>, not the total area,&nbsp;<math alttext="in mm Superscript 2"><msup><mtext>in&nbsp;mm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, of the circles.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. This is the sum of the diameters,&nbsp;<math alttext="in mm"><mtext>in&nbsp;mm</mtext></math>,&nbsp;of circles <math alttext="upper K"><mi>K</mi>
