    ($SATQ$9912e19f$SATQ$, $SATQ$7d655176-161a-479c-b588-adb3cf6c69c2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Lines, angles, and triangles$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Triangles <math alttext="upper E upper F upper G"><mrow>
	<mi>E</mi>
	<mi>F</mi>
	<mi>G</mi>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="upper J upper K upper L"><mrow>
	<mi>J</mi>
	<mi>K</mi>
	<mi>L</mi>
</mrow>
</math> are congruent, where <math alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi>
</math>, <math alttext="upper F"><mi>F</mi>
</math>, and <math alttext="upper G"><mi>G</mi>
</math> correspond to <math alttext="upper J"><mi>J</mi>
</math>, <math alttext="upper K"><mi>K</mi>
</math>, and <math alttext="upper L"><mi>L</mi>
</math>, respectively. The measure of angle <math alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi>
</math> is&nbsp;<math alttext="45 degree"><mrow><mn>45</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;and the measure of angle <math alttext="upper F"><mi>F</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="20 degree"><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure of angle <math alttext="upper J"><mi>J</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"acd321af-5eb2-492c-9776-2d6fb5cab26f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"20 degree\"><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"4b0995e7-c7d2-4636-9daf-f115ff2fb359","content":"<p><math alttext=\"45 degree\"><mrow><mn>45</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"1e06cd08-c2dd-4f29-bbb0-0e17ade3c53c","content":"<p><math alttext=\"135 degree\"><mrow><mn>135</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"665c25d1-ae13-4ca4-b455-0ab67a0e2b13","content":"<p><math alttext=\"160 degree\"><mrow><mn>160</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4b0995e7-c7d2-4636-9daf-f115ff2fb359"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. It's given that triangles <math alttext="upper E upper F upper G"><mrow>
<mi>E</mi>
<mi>F</mi>
<mi>G</mi>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="upper J upper K upper L"><mrow>
<mi>J</mi>
<mi>K</mi>
<mi>L</mi>
</mrow>
</math> are congruent such that angle <math alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi>
</math> corresponds to angle <math alttext="upper J"><mi>J</mi>
</math>. Corresponding angles of congruent triangles are congruent, so angle <math alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi>
</math> and angle <math alttext="upper J"><mi>J</mi>
</math> must be congruent. Therefore, if the measure of angle <math alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="45 degree"><mn>45</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, then the measure of angle <math alttext="upper J"><mi>J</mi>
</math> is also <math alttext="45 degree"><mn>45</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. This is the measure of angle <math alttext="upper K"><mi>K</mi>
</math>, not angle <math alttext="upper J"><mi>J</mi>
</math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
