    ($SATQ$9adb86ed$SATQ$, $SATQ$22d64b0b-de62-4d03-bfd1-a0f9bfc7fb43$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Circles$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Points <math alttext="upper Q"><mi>Q</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="upper R"><mi>R</mi>
</math> lie on a circle with center <math alttext="upper P"><mi>P</mi>
</math>. The radius of this circle is <math alttext="9"><mn>9</mn>
</math> inches. Triangle <math alttext="upper P upper Q upper R"><mrow>
	<mi>P</mi>
	<mi>Q</mi>
	<mi>R</mi>
</mrow>
</math> has a perimeter of <math alttext="31"><mn>31</mn>
</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of&nbsp;<math alttext="line segment upper Q upper R"><mover><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b6065b71-3985-4704-b100-e4df0c4f13eb","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"13 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>13</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math></p>"},{"id":"41be3f7c-0fe0-4ceb-a3ad-03ca41540a6c","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d4198c92-513d-4eaa-866f-a62e46aad366","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math></p>"},{"id":"360b9cc3-ca1e-4220-950b-bc4ccc539e1d","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["41be3f7c-0fe0-4ceb-a3ad-03ca41540a6c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. Since it's given that <math alttext="upper P"><mi>P</mi>
</math> is the center of a circle with a radius of <math alttext="9"><mn>9</mn>
</math> inches, and that points <math alttext="upper Q"><mi>Q</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="upper R"><mi>R</mi>
</math> lie on that circle, it follows that <math alttext="line segment upper P upper Q"><mover><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> and <math alttext="line segment upper R upper P"><mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> of triangle <math alttext="upper P upper Q upper R"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> each have a length of <math alttext="9"><mn>9</mn>
</math> inches. Let the length of <math alttext="line segment upper Q upper R"><mover><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>&macr;</mo></mover></math> be <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> inches. It follows that the perimeter of triangle <math alttext="upper P upper Q upper R"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math alttext="9 plus 9 plus x"><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></math> inches. Since it's given that the perimeter of triangle <math alttext="upper P upper Q upper R"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math alttext="31"><mn>31</mn>
</math> inches, it follows that <math alttext="9 plus 9 plus x equals 31"><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>31</mn></math>, or <math alttext="18 plus x equals 31"><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>31</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext="18"><mn>18</mn>
